## Being debt-free is better

Is it good to go into debt? Not always. Having debts means being a slave. Every installment you have to pay becomes a stone in your shoe. It causes many problems. So if you have debts, you should try to get out of them as soon as possible. If you are going to take on debt, do it in a planned, strategic, and controlled way, so that your slavery is brief.

The following statement written by José Antonio Emmanuel in his pamphlet Anarchy Explained to Children (Emmanuel 1931) says:

> […] Aspire to be free and may the longing for your freedom embrace everyone. Do not enslave anyone. Neither birds nor any living being can you lock up with impunity. Open the doors of all the cages, file down the bars of all the prisons, where –like the caged bird– human beings suffer and endure. Be free and make others free, along with you. Open the doors of your heart so that all the vices, all the defects that managed to slip in, may leave it. Be free and be pure: neither keep slaves, nor become a slave. […]

— _José Antonio Emmanuel_

Neither keep slaves, nor become one. By taking on a debt you automatically become a slave to the person or entity that lent you the money. Therefore you should never lend money to friends, relatives, and other associates, since you will be their slave or you will turn them into your slaves.

## Do not lend money

A true, quality friendship is far more valuable than any sum of money. When a friend or relative needs financial help and you have the capacity to help, you should never lend them the money. Ask them for a favor in return, have them sell you something you want or need, or simply give it as a gift.

If, for example, you agree on a payment date and the friend does not deliver, the friendship will be affected. The friend will feel ashamed for not delivering and friendships of many years can disappear completely. If a friend owes you little or a lot of money and you consider their friendship worth keeping, forgive the debt and agree on a non-monetary form of repayment. If you consider that the person does not deserve to be called a friend, forgive the debt, let them know, and continue your life without that person; consider that you paid that amount of money never to see them again.

What matters is eliminating this master — slave relationship that arises from having debts with people in our circle.

We can learn a little from the Roman sage Cicero.

> Certainly hateful is the race of men who throw back in one's face the favors they have lent; favors must be kept in the memory of the one who receives them, never of the one who lends them.

> And what greater folly is there than when many, thanks to their wealth and position, can acquire everything that money can buy: horses, slaves, exquisite garments, precious vessels; and yet do not acquire friends, which is the finest and most beautiful possession of life?

— _Cicero_

Or also from the words of Polonius to his son Laertes, in William Shakespeare's play, Hamlet.

> […] The friends you have, whose loyalty has been proven, grapple them to your soul with hoops of steel, and do not waste time with brave fellows of words, freshly hatched from the shell.

> Do not lend or borrow, because by lending you can lose both the money and the friend, and by borrowing you spoil the good management of your affairs. […]

— _Polonius, Hamlet, William Shakespeare_.

In conclusion, if you have debts with relatives or friends, try to settle them as soon as possible. *Clear accounts preserve friendship.*

## Do not be a guarantor

To take out a loan or buy something on credit they may ask for a guarantor. This is because they distrust that the person requesting the loan will pay it. Being a guarantor means that you have enough money and the responsibility to take on a debt that is not yours. Most people are careless with their money and it is likely they will not pay this debt on time. If you are a guarantor you must be prepared to assume the entire debt.

## Shop smart

It is not always necessary to buy the newest, latest-generation notebook, phone, or car. Many times you can acquire second-hand or open-box products, or at good prices. Many products are just as good up to 5 years (or more) after their debut; with a little cleaning and perhaps a replacement part they will be as useful as any new product. Do not be fooled by store “deals” or by novelties that will be obsolete six months from now. A real deal is when you price things properly and generate your own offer. A clear example is bioequivalent medicines at small pharmacies versus brand-name medicines at big pharmacies. The savings are very significant.

The concept of cheap or expensive is dangerous. Prioritize better-quality, durable items over disposable and ephemeral things. Why is the concept of cheap or expensive dangerous? Because the perceived value of an item depends on your situation at the time of purchase. If, for example, you earn over a million pesos a month, a 50-thousand-peso ticket to an event will seem reasonable to you, but if you only earn 250 thousand pesos a month, that same ticket has a prohibitive cost and will seem extremely expensive. So it is not good to think of prices as cheap or expensive; simply think about whether your current budget allows it or not.

For example, there are people who spend loads of hours or even days standing in line to buy a product on promotion or at its debut. Frankly, I consider that saving yourself a couple of pesos does not justify losing that time. Instead of spending a whole day in line you could do some work as an Uber driver or Cornershop courier and earn the money you were supposed to be saving.

Time is more important than money. So if something saves you time and costs a little more, it is a good option.

Buying better-quality things with a longer useful life is more worthwhile than buying low-quality things. Higher-quality items usually tend to have a higher price. Try to get the best quality your budget allows.

Also evaluate whether you really need to buy. Many times we start accumulating things we never use. There is also the problem of buying to show off or for some impulsive reason. Just because your coworkers, neighbors, friends, or relatives have the latest car model does not mean you must have it too. Just because a product has a lower price does not mean you need to have it.

I would recommend reading the work _Marie Kondo: The Magic of Order_ and the documentary _Minimalism on Netflix_.

## Credit Cards

In most cases having a credit card is not necessary. Paying cash can even get you bigger discounts, if you are a good negotiator. But there are certain cases where having a credit card can be useful. Below, some points to consider before using a credit card are detailed.

### Pay everything off at the end of the month

If you cannot pay the full monthly installment, you are better off not using the credit card. Late-payment interest can devastate any budget and bring plenty of unnecessary drama.

Now, if the installment is very high, you can pay the minimum amount. Let's say you owe `CLP $600.000` (UF 21.75) and the minimum payment for the month can be close to `CLP $80.000` (UF 2.90).

If you pay those eighty thousand pesos, no interest will be added to the debt. Now you will owe `CLP $520.000` and you can pay the minimum amount the following month, until you have enough money to pay in full.

Ideally you pay as much as possible so the minimum amount is low and you can pay it. Refinancing debts is not worthwhile. They may offer you that, but you will add an extra amount of money just to buy a little time.

### Five times the value

Before buying something with the credit card, check whether your monthly budget can pay five times the value of the item you are buying. This is to avoid compulsive purchases. With access to credit, people tend to buy more. If you were to pay in cash, you would think twice before handing over money you could use for other purposes.

For example, if a coffee costs `CLP $2.000`, you should have at least a `CLP $10.000` bill in your pocket before thinking of buying it on credit.

### Benefits

Credit cards usually have agreements with different stores and organizations that might offer discounts or other benefits. However, I recommend first checking small, local options over big stores, since if you pay cash at local shops you can ask for a bigger discount and, in passing, you will be helping a family rather than a corporation. Entities that offer credit cards usually offer some prize, points, or discount for getting and using the card, but in general they are barely significant and not worth it. If you are going to have a credit card, make sure it has the greatest quantity and quality of benefits possible.

### The 15th of Each Month

If you buy after the 15th, you will pay the installment at the end of the following month. Example: you buy something on August 16th and you pay the installment on September 30th. That gives you up to 45 days to gather the money and pay the full installment.

### Is more than one a good idea?

Having more than one credit card is not worthwhile. Get quotes and compare; choose the one with the most benefits that allows online and foreign purchases. Although for most purchases it is more convenient to use cash or a debit card. Having more cards may give you more purchasing options and associated benefits, but it is also a new responsibility that will take up your time. If you will have more cards, organize them so that you always pay installments and maintenance fees on time.

### Few installments

The more installments, the lower the amount paid monthly, but the higher the final total. Consider how much you will ultimately pay for the purchase (including maintenance fees) before deciding on installments. I prefer to pay everything without installments.

### One at a time

My recommendation is to use one card until you maximize the possible benefits. If a friend or relative wants to buy something and has the cash, you can use your card to get a discount and pay immediately. This will let you accumulate more benefits without getting into debt and will give the buyer a considerable discount.

### Self-control

If you lose control or are careless when buying things, it is better not to have a credit card and to use only cash.

### Safety first

Having a credit card is synonymous with the risk of charges from stolen data, even if you have never used the card. I recommend renewing the card every 3 months to prevent some website or institution from having a security breach and the information leaking out to make payments in your name. Cloning the card's information is also possible. It is advisable to buy a protective sleeve to block RFID readers as well.

Renewing the card also gets some services you are not using removed. It helps you analyze whether you really use that service before entering your card details again.

Prioritize an institution that lets you replace the card easily and at no cost.

## Stories

### The cursed t-shirt

A gentleman told me that a charge of `CLP $200.000` mysteriously appeared on his account, from a country that did not even speak Spanish.

This was suspicious since he was not well versed in online shopping and did not use his card very often, nor did he have that kind of money.

What happened was that his son had bought a t-shirt in an online store using his card, but after a while the store suffered a security breach and the data of many customers was released.

This allowed fraudulent purchases with the card. Unable to pay, he ended up in DICOM (2019). He managed to get out by paying the debt after a while and with much effort.

### The ghost card

A lady told me that she once accepted a credit card, thinking she would only use it in cases of extreme necessity, so it was still in its unopened package. Then the Redbanc security breach happened (Cooperativa 2019) and her data was on the list of those affected. She was charged `CLP $1.000.000` for expenses at a hotel in Brazil, when she did not have that money or even a passport to leave the country. Fortunately she managed to appeal to SERNAC (2019) in time and her debt was forgiven.

## Budget for Emergencies

The credit card is not for paying a medical emergency, car repairs, or out-of-budget purchases. For emergencies you must always have a separate, dedicated fund.

## Knowing How to Use Credit

Normally when you can meet the points stated above and when you need to make purchases that carry some risk. It is easier to undo a credit card transaction than a debit or cash one. If you have to make a payment, you have some time to reverse the transaction and recover the money in case of fraud or dissatisfaction with the service or product purchased.

### Mike Sources' example

When Mike Sources (BitNation Citizen) was in university (circa 2003), a bank was offering credit cards to students. Many got theirs and spent their limit (CLP $100.000) on clothes, sneakers, and other unnecessary purchases. Mike noticed that many people at his university gathered to play Pokémon cards and, instead of buying clothes, he bought complete boxes of cards at a good price and started selling the cards individually (at a low cost, greed is the enemy of cash flow). He studied the conditions for interest-free installments and paid the full monthly amount. Not only could he settle the debt on time, he also generated a profit that let him cover much of his student expenses and some luxuries.

## Banks

People tend to have an emotional attachment to their cards, especially if they are the first ones they were able to get. Financial institutions take advantage of this, since people will be more loyal to the first institution that manages to seduce them. Having a good card is important, so do not hesitate to switch institutions if you find a better offer and benefits.

## Emergency Fund

The emergency fund is used to survive in case of an accident, theft, malfunction, or simply Murphy's Law (if something can go wrong, it will). The recommendation is to determine the minimum you need to survive for a month and multiply it by 3. That way you can live 3 months without income, if the case calls for it. I would recommend having at least approximately `CLP $500.000` (20 UF (2019)), although it will depend on your needs.

Having an emergency fund helps give you peace of mind. In an unforeseen event, you will not need to go into debt (or only into minor debt) to get out of that situation.

Ideally you should put this savings in an account separate from the one you normally use, mainly to avoid spending this money on daily purchases. Look for a way to get as close as possible to a 3% annual return (CPI value (2019)). That way your savings will keep their purchasing power.

This fund must always be kept at its ideal amount, in case one day it must be used. The amount can grow, but beyond 3 months of living expenses, in my opinion just saving it is not very advisable; it should be invested or used for some purpose.

## Getting Out of Debt

There are multiple strategies for getting out of debt. The important thing is to be organized and methodical. For example there is the “Snowball” strategy (ordering from lowest to highest value) promoted by Dave Ramsey, in which the smallest debts are prioritized and the money accumulates until they can all be paid off. Another strategy is the “Avalanche” (ordering from highest to lowest interest), where the debts with the highest interest are prioritized and paid downward like an avalanche.

You must be aware that no strategy is a silver bullet and it must be adapted to each person's context; preferably seek help from an accountant friend or a financial professional.

## The Bennedetto Budget

Having a budget is very important to avoid falling into financial problems. You must plan where your money goes. If you do not manage it properly, it will go anywhere and we will end up with drama.

The budget system that has caught my attention the most is `Bennedetto` by Alex Recker.

`Bennedetto` is a minimalist system for monitoring expenses, inspired by the Civilization video game. Basically it lets you monitor income and expenses, giving as a result “You have $X per day to spend.”

Among the benefits of using this system we can name:

- Requires little estimation: No long-term projections or estimates are needed. What you get are results based on facts and data.

- Quick feedback: Every day you can know how much budget you have to spend and monitor your expenses against that daily value.

- Flexible: Everything is based on the data you enter. Results and values can vary depending on current conditions, adapting to the context.

### How it works

To use this system you can use a spreadsheet or software like Excel, Numbers, LibreOffice, Gnumeric, or the Bennedetto project itself.

```github
arecker/bennedetto | Sistema minimalista de presupuesto inspirado en el videojuego Civilization
```

### How does it work?

This sheet stores all fixed expenses separated by the following columns `Name`, `Amount (CLP)`, `Days`, `Amount/Day (CLP)`, `Amount/Month (CLP)` (the amount divided by the number of days). For example, if we have a monthly rent of `CLP $200.000`, a Netflix subscription costing `CLP $5.000` monthly, and a .cl domain costing `CLP $10.000` a year, the table would look like the following.

``` =html
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Amount (CLP)</th><th>Days</th><th>Amount/Day (CLP)</th><th>Amount/Month (CLP)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Netflix</td><td>5.000</td><td>30</td><td>167</td><td>5.000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rent</td><td>200.000</td><td>30</td><td>6.700</td><td>200.000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Nic.cl</td><td>10.000</td><td>365</td><td>28</td><td>834</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

To the fixed expenses table you must add all recurring expenses, for example: rent, internet, electricity, water, gas, transportation, savings accounts, social security contributions, investments, among others.

### Fixed Income

Just like the expenses, you must build a sheet of fixed income. Let's assume a value close to the Chilean minimum wage. `CLP $300.000` (UF 10.87).

``` =html
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Amount (CLP)</th><th>Days</th><th>Amount/Day (CLP)</th><th>Amount/Month (CLP)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Salary</td><td>300.000</td><td>30</td><td>10.000</td><td>300.000</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

### Movements

This is the sheet that must be constantly updated with daily income and expenses. For example, you can sell something and have earned extra income this month, which would positively affect the available daily amount.

The necessary columns are `Name`, `Amount`, `Comment`, `Date`. The amount must be positive if it is income, and negative if it is an expense.

``` =html
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Amount (CLP)</th><th>Comment</th><th>Date</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Socks purchase</td><td>-3.000</td><td>A new pair of socks.</td><td>September 30, 2019</td></tr>
    <tr><td>TV sale</td><td>20.000</td><td>An old TV was sold to make room</td><td>September 29, 2019</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

So we have `CLP $17.000`.

### Totals

This sheet contains the formulas to obtain the available money.

``` =html
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Amount (CLP)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Monthly Total</td><td>=SUM(Amount/Month Fixed Income) + SUM(Amount Movements) - SUM(Amount/Month Fixed Expenses)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Annual Total</td><td>= Monthly Total * 12</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weekly Total</td><td>= Monthly Total / 7</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Daily Total</td><td>= Monthly Total / 30</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

In our case the table would end up with the following values:

``` =html
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Amount (CLP)</th><th>Comment</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Monthly Total</td><td>CLP $111.166</td><td>300.000 (Fixed Income) + 17.000 (Movements) - 205.834 (Fixed Expenses)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Annual Total</td><td>CLP $1.333.992</td><td>Monthly Total * 12</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weekly Total</td><td>CLP $15.880</td><td>Monthly Total / 7</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Daily Total</td><td>CLP $3.705</td><td>Monthly Total / 30</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

So we can determine that we have CLP $3.705 daily to spend without going over budget.

This will let us organize ourselves month by month. So building a monthly spreadsheet will help us keep our income and expenses under control.

## Conclusion

Keeping our income and expenses in order is key to achieving a healthy financial situation. This article can be summarized in the following points:

- Pay your debts and keep them under control.
- Buy quality items. Learn to price things properly before buying.
- Organize your expenses and use credit options wisely.
- Have a budget and try to always stay within its limits.

